course: world
lesson: 125
title: BC and AD, dinosaurs and pyramids, and timelines
level: primary-4
stars: 3
explain: Our year numbers count from a starting point that a monk worked out about 1,500 years ago. Years after it are AD ("Anno Domini", Latin for "in the year of the Lord"), also written CE; years before it are BC ("before Christ"), also written BCE. BC years count down towards the start like a rocket countdown, and AD years count up.
explain: There is no year 0: 1 BC is followed straight by AD 1. So to find the gap from a BC year to an AD year, add the two numbers and take away 1. Julius Caesar was killed in 44 BC, which is 44 + 2026 − 1 = 2,069 years before 2026.
explain: Deep time plays tricks. The Great Pyramid, finished about 2560 BC, is about 4,500 years old; Cleopatra lived closer in time to the first Moon landing than to its building, and the last woolly mammoths were still alive on Wrangel Island, in the Arctic, after it was built. T. rex lived closer in time to us than to Stegosaurus, and no human ever met a dinosaur, apart from the birds, which are dinosaurs' living relatives.
explain: A timeline puts events in order on a line with a scale. Squash Earth's 4.5 billion years into one day of 24 hours, and the dinosaurs die out about 21 minutes before midnight, while humans arrive only about 6 seconds before.
explain: Some old dates are traditions rather than measurements, like Kyiv's founding in AD 482, and big claims, like aliens building Stonehenge, need evidence: archaeologists have found the tools, homes and bones of the people who really built it.
fact: BC (or BCE) years count backwards before year 1; AD (or CE) years count forwards from year 1.
fact: There is no year 0: 1 BC is followed directly by AD 1.
fact: To find how many years lie between a BC year and an AD year, add the two numbers and take away 1.
fact: 500 BC and AD 500 are 999 years apart, and 10 BC and AD 10 are 19 years apart.
fact: Julius Caesar was killed in 44 BC, 2,069 years before 2026.
fact: The Great Pyramid of Giza was finished about 2560 BC, about 4,500 years ago.
fact: Non-bird dinosaurs died out about 66 million years ago; humans appeared only about 300,000 years ago, so no human ever saw one.
fact: Birds are the living relatives of the dinosaurs.
fact: Stegosaurus lived about 150 million years ago and T. rex about 68 to 66 million years ago, so T. rex lived closer in time to us than to Stegosaurus.
fact: The last woolly mammoths, on Wrangel Island in the Arctic, died out about 4,000 years ago, after the Great Pyramid was built.
fact: On a timeline of Earth's 4.5 billion years squashed into 24 hours, humans appear only about 6 seconds before midnight.
fact: Kyiv's traditional founding date is AD 482, which makes it 1,544 years old in 2026; the date comes from old stories.
fact: On a 1-metre timeline of the last 2,000 years, each centimetre stands for 20 years.
fact: Stonehenge was built and rebuilt by people from about 3000 BC to about 1500 BC, using ropes, wood and picks made of deer antler.
fact: Cleopatra lived about 2,000 years ago, dying in 30 BC, so in her time the Great Pyramid was already about 2,500 years old.
exercise: What does BC mean? | Before Christ: years counted backwards before year 1 | Before Calendars: years when nobody wrote any dates at all | British Century: the years when old Britain was ruled by Rome | Big Count: the years that came after AD 1000, counted up
exercise: What does AD stand for? | Anno Domini, Latin for "in the year of the Lord" | After Dinosaurs, the years since they all died out | Ancient Days, the years before Rome was ever built | All Dates, every single year that was ever counted
exercise: Which letters do many books use instead of BC and AD? | BCE and CE | BBC and ABC | BT and AT | OS and NS
exercise: Which year came straight after 1 BC? | AD 1 | Year 0 | AD 2 | 2 BC
exercise: How many years are there from 10 BC to AD 10? | 19 | 20 | 0 | 21
exercise: How many years apart are 500 BC and AD 500? | 999 | 0 | 1,000 | 500
exercise: Julius Caesar was killed in 44 BC. How long before 2026 was that? | 2,069 years | 1,982 years | 2,070 years | 2,026 years
exercise: About how old is the Great Pyramid of Giza? | About 4,500 years | About 2,000 years | About 450 years | About 45,000 years
exercise: When did the non-bird dinosaurs die out? | About 66 million years ago | About 66,000 years ago | About 6,000 years ago | About 300,000 years ago
exercise: Which animals alive today are the living relatives of the dinosaurs? | Birds | Frogs and toads | Sharks | Butterflies
exercise: Which dinosaur lived closer in time to us than to Stegosaurus? | T. rex | Diplodocus | Allosaurus | Brachiosaurus
exercise: When did the last woolly mammoths die out? | About 4,000 years ago, on an Arctic island | About 66 million years ago, with the dinosaurs | About 400 years ago, in Russian zoos | About a million years ago, everywhere at once
exercise: On a 24-hour "day" of Earth's history, when do humans appear? | About 6 seconds before midnight | About 6 hours before midnight | Just after midnight at the start | At about midday
exercise: Kyiv's traditional founding date is AD 482. How old is it in 2026? | 1,544 years | 2,508 years | 1,444 years | 482 years
exercise: On Mila's 1-metre timeline of 2,000 years, how far from the end does her birth in 2016 go? | Half a centimetre | 10 centimetres | 1 centimetre | 50 centimetres
exercise: Who built Stonehenge? | People, using tools like ropes, wood and antler picks | Aliens, using flying machines | Giants who lived in ancient Britain | The ancient Egyptians, who sailed all the way to Britain to build it
test: Zoe: "500 BC in one book and AD 500 in another, same year innit?" | No, they're 999 years apart, either side of year 1 | Yes, BC and AD are just two different names for one year | No, they're exactly 1,000 years apart, 500 and 500 | No, they're only 500 years apart, because both say 500
test: Theo: "What happened in year 0, sir?" Teacher: "Trick question." Why? | There was no year 0; 1 BC is followed by AD 1 | Year 0 was skipped in 1752 | Nothing happened, so it was left out of books | Year 0 lasted only one day
test: Jack, from Glasgow: "Caesar got done in in 44 BC, so that's 1,982 year ago, aye?" ("Got done in" means was killed.) What's right? | 2,069 years: add 44 and 2026, then take away 1 | Yes, 2026 take away 44 is 1,982 | 2,070 years: just add 44 and 2026 | 44 years, since BC counts from now
test: Mia: "The pyramids are like 2,000 years old, no?" Her sister: "Way older, babe." Who's right? | The sister: about 4,500 years | Mia: about 2,000 years | Neither: about 450 years | Neither: about 45,000 years
test: Liam: "Cavemen fought dinosaurs, it was in a cartoon, so it's real." His sister: "Nah, that's proper wrong." ("Proper" means very.) Who's right? | She is: dinosaurs died out about 66 million years before humans | Liam is: cavemen hunted T. rex for food | Liam is, but only small dinosaurs | She is, because dinosaurs never really existed and the bones are fake
test: Amir: "T. rex closer to us than to Stegosaurus? Skibidi nonsense!" (He means rubbish.) Check with numbers. | About 83 million years from Stegosaurus to T. rex, about 66 million from T. rex to us | They lived at the same time, so it is nonsense | T. rex lived 150 million years ago, before Stegosaurus | Stegosaurus lived just 1,000 years before T. rex
test: Ruby: "Egyptians and mammoths at the same time? Wild." ("Wild" means amazing.) Is it true? | Yes, the last mammoths lived on an Arctic island until about 4,000 years ago | No, mammoths died out with the dinosaurs | No, mammoths only appeared on Earth after the Romans had built all their roads | Yes, the Egyptians kept mammoths as pets
test: Ben squashes Earth's whole history into one day. About when do the dinosaurs die out on his clock? | About 21 minutes before midnight | At breakfast time | Just after midnight at the very start | Exactly at noon
test: Olena: "Kyiv was founded in 482, so it's over 1,500 years old?" What's careful and true? | 1,544 years by the traditional date, which comes from old stories | Exactly 1,544 years, proven to the very day | Only 482 years old | 2,508 years old
test: Mila draws 2,000 years on 1 metre. Her mate: "Your birth's gonna take up half the line, bruv." What's right? | It's just half a centimetre from the end | Yes, half the line, so a whole 50 centimetres | It's 10 centimetres from the end of the line | It's right at the very start of the whole line
test: Josh's mate: "Aliens built Stonehenge, 100%." Josh: "Nah, people with ropes and wood, ya muppet." What's the best reason Josh is right? | Archaeologists found the builders' tools, homes and bones | Aliens are too short to lift such big, heavy stones on their own | Stonehenge was only built last century, so no aliens were needed | Stonehenge is made of wood, not stone, so it was easy for anyone
test: A cartoon shows Cleopatra watching the Great Pyramid being built. Fix it. | The pyramid was already over 2,000 years old in her time | She did watch it, in 2560 BC | The pyramid was built after she died | She built it herself in AD 30
exam: Ascertain the number of years elapsing between the year 44 BC and the year AD 2026. | Two thousand and sixty-nine | One thousand nine hundred and eighty-two | Two thousand and seventy | Two thousand and twenty-six
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the year designated zero. | No such year exists, the year 1 BC being immediately succeeded by AD 1 | It is the year in which the Julian calendar was first instituted by Caesar | It is the first year of the Common Era | It is the year of the completion of the Great Pyramid
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the relation in time between human beings and the non-bird dinosaurs? | The dinosaurs died out some 66 million years before human beings appeared | The two lived side by side for several thousand years | The dinosaurs died out approximately 4,000 years ago | Human beings appeared before the dinosaurs
exam: Given a timeline of one metre representing two thousand years, determine the length representing a single century. | Five centimetres | One centimetre | Ten centimetres | Twenty centimetres
